I might use a function to create the observers.

#+begin_src R
  mapply(list(
    oneSamplePopulationMean =
      list(userdata = w,
           userdataReactive = x,
           dataTableOutput = y,
           dataTabePanel = z),
    twoSampleIndependentPopulationMeans =
      list(userdata = w,
           userdataReactive = x,
           dataTableOutput = y,
           dataTabePanel = z)
    ...
  ),
  function(userdata, userdataReactive, dataTableOutput, dataTabPanel) {
    observeEvent(input[[userdata]], {
      output[[dataTableOutput]] <- renderDataTable(userdataReactive())
      show(dataTabPanel)
    })
  })
#+end_src


* Guide for =br()=, the line break tag-creating function            :ARCHIVE:
:PROPERTIES:
:ARCHIVE_TIME: 2026-07-06 Mon 12:32
:ARCHIVE_FILE: ~/CougarStats/R/statInfr.org
:ARCHIVE_CATEGORY: statInfr
:END:
HTML is not a line-oriented markup language like others. Line breaks may be
freely mixed with content without terminating a line of prose. See the following
example.

#+begin_src html
  <body>
    <p>
      My first paragraph with some
      <strong>bold</strong>
      text!
    </p>
  </body>
#+end_src

In this example the usage of the =strong= tag is emphasized by placing the tag
and its content on a separate line from other content. The line break and the
interceding whitespace do not affect the text content of the =p= tag (for
paragraphing content). The final content is rendered as: "My first paragraph
with some *bold* text!". The example could have had a source formatting like the
following, and still been rendered the same way by the browser.

#+begin_src html :tangle /tmp/test.html
  <div>
  <p>My first paragraph with some <strong>bold</strong> text!</p>
  <p>
    My
    second
    paragraph with some <strong>bold</strong> text!
  </p>
  <p>
    My
    third
    paragraph
    with
    some
    <strong>     bold     </strong>
    text!</p>
  </div>
#+end_src

The browser concatenates the inner content and renders the text on as few lines
as required, wrapping according to the default text-breaking and -wrapping rules
of the browser and web standards. To write verse, rather than prose, you may use
tags like =pre=, but sometimes =pre= is too much formatting and you want to
enforce a line break without dictating the line wrapping and text-splitting
rules for the rest of the tag. That is where you use the =br= tag, the break
tag.

#+begin_src html :tangle /tmp/test.html
  <div>
    <p>
      My first paragraph with some
      <br>
      <strong>bold</strong>
      text!
    </p>
  </div>
#+end_src

The =br= tag is not used for vertical spacing between lines or other HTML
content; which can be shown with the following example.

#+begin_src html :tangle /tmp/test.html
  <div style="line-height: 2.5;">
    <p>
      My first paragraph with some
      <br>
      <strong>bold</strong>
      text!
    </p>
  </div>
#+end_src

=br= is *never* to be used to create vertical spacing between elements, as *it
does not create vertical spacing between elements*, it breaks a line of text
only.


